nce turns managed selection on is docs/agents-enabled.\"","+code_symbol = \"load_registry/resolve_enabled/entry_route/_row_of/parse_env/select\"","+detail = \"Parses pair rows and enable tokens; resolves versions; merges Env; selects by tier, family, cooldown, and order with legacy Provider compatibility. Selection tiers up and never down, honors family preference with documented fallback, resolves a version-less token deterministically, and reports no available route honestly rather than guessing. The pair rows are docs/agents.toml's; the consent surface whose presence turns managed selection on is docs/agents-enabled. Each enable-list line is one entry; selection, preference and cooldowns carry that entry rather than its bare route id, and a registry row is read only through the entry's route. An unqualified entry key is its route id; a qualified entry key is ROUTE@ACCOUNT.\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-046)\""," status = \"Approved\""," component = \"CMP-008\""]},{"oldStart":701,"oldLines":8,"newStart":703,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-155\"]"," title = \"Dual-plan planner pair and fallback\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/agent_route.py\"","-code_symbol = \"planner_pair/planner_fallback\"","-detail = \"Selects two fresh planner sessions across families where possible and applies the bounded nonresponse fallback with reason codes. A one-family or single-row pool still yields two fresh sessions with reason codes; a runtime nonresponse excludes the dead family; a no-responder outcome is explicit, never silent.\"","+code_symbol = \"planner_pair/planner_fallback/PlannerSession.model_id\"","+detail = \"Selects two fresh planner sessions across families where possible and applies the bounded nonresponse fallback with reason codes. A one-family or single-row pool still yields two fresh sessions with reason codes; a runtime nonresponse excludes the dead family; a no-responder outcome is explicit, never silent. Each session carries the enabled entry it was routed to, and its model is the registry route that entry selects.\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-072)\""," status = \"Approved\""," component = \"CMP-008\""]},{"oldStart":2985,"oldLines":8,"newStart":2987,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-222\"]"," title = \"One adapter per provider runner captures its structured output\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/session_adapters.py\"","-code_symbol = \"adapter_for/CodexAdapter/OpencodeAdapter/json_events\"","-detail = \"\"\"adapter_for(argv) picks the adapter by the basename prefix of the executable argv launches (claude, codex, opencode, case-insensitive), else a plain adapter that changes nothing. A stand-in gets the plain adapter, which leaves argv, final text and usage as the claude-shaped reader returns them. prepare(argv) adds the runner's structured-output flags once: codex gets both --json and an -o/--output-last-message temp file; opencode gets --format json; claude's route emits stream-json. run_session captures the whole stream; the adapter, not the launch, reads the final text: codex's is the last-message file on a zero exit and the stream otherwise, opencode's is the text of the last step that spoke, or the stream as it came when no JSON event parsed. raw_usage(stream) returns the usage-bearing events (codex's events carrying a usage object, opencode's step_finish events) as a JSON array whose members are the runner's own lines, byte for byte, and the usage record stores it in the raw-usage column. An attached sitting keeps its argv as written.\"\"\"","+code_symbol = \"adapter_for/CodexAdapter/OpencodeAdapter/json_events/HOME_VARIABLES/TOKEN_VARIABLES/cli_named/home_env\"","+detail = \"\"\"adapter_for(argv) picks the adapter by the basename prefix of the executable argv launches (claude, codex, opencode, case-insensitive), else a plain adapter that changes nothing. A stand-in gets the plain adapter, which leaves argv, final text and usage as the claude-shaped reader returns them. prepare(argv) adds the runner's structured-output flags once: codex gets both --json and an -o/--output-last-message temp file; opencode gets --format json; claude's route emits stream-json. run_session captures the whole stream; the adapter, not the launch, reads the final text: codex's is the last-message file on a zero exit and the stream otherwise, opencode's is the text of the last step that spoke, or the stream as it came when no JSON event parsed. raw_usage(stream) returns the usage-bearing events (codex's events carrying a usage object, opencode's step_finish events) as a JSON array whose members are the runner's own lines, byte for byte, and the usage record stores it in the raw-usage column. An attached sitting keeps its argv as written. The adapter receives the call’s composed environment and does not select, replace or infer an account home.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"A route whose final text replaces its captured stream loses the usage-bearing events in that stream, and a route asked for no structured output has no usage-bearing events to keep. Choosing the adapter by the program launched rather than by the routed family keeps the flags with the grammar that needs them. Keeping each usage line as written, rather than a re-serialised object, is what lets a mapping later found wrong be re-derived from the record.\"\"\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-262)\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":2997,"oldLines":8,"newStart":2999,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-222\"]"," title = \"Context occupancy is read from the latest request's prompt\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/session_adapters.py\"","-code_symbol = \"ClaudeAdapter.context/CodexAdapter.context/OpencodeAdapter.context\"","-detail = \"\"\"context(stream, env, session_id) returns (session_id, used, window, pct) for the session's final model request, each field empty where the runner does not report it. claude: used is the last assistant event's message.usage input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens, falling back to the result's last usage.iterations entry; the window is the contextWindow of the modelUsage entry for the model that request named, so a background model's entry beside it is never taken. codex: the exec --json turn usage is cumulative over the thread and is never read as occupancy; used is the last rollout token_count event's info.last_token_usage.input_tokens and the window its info.model_context_window, read from the rollout file named for exactly this thread id under the launch environment's CODEX_HOME or, when that is unset, under codex's own default home, and blank when no rollout for that thread id exists there. opencode: used is the last step_finish event's part.tokens input + cache.read + cache.write; opencode reports no window, so window and percent stay blank. pct is the rounded percent when used and a positive window are both counts. The session service writes the four fields to the session-id, context-used, context-window and context-pct columns for every call.\"\"\"","+code_symbol = \"ClaudeAdapter.context/CodexAdapter.context/OpencodeAdapter.context/_codex_home/_codex_rollout\"","+detail = \"\"\"context(stream, env, session_id) returns (session_id, used, window, pct) for the session's final model request, each field empty where the runner does not report it. claude: used is the last assistant event's message.usage input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens, falling back to the result's last usage.iterations entry; the window is the contextWindow of the modelUsage entry for the model that request named, so a background model's entry beside it is never taken. codex: the exec --json turn usage is cumulative over the thread and is never read as occupancy; used is the last rollout token_count event's info.last_token_usage.input_tokens and the window its info.model_context_window, read from the rollout file named for exactly this thread id under the launch environment's CODEX_HOME or, when that is unset, under codex's own default home, and blank when no rollout for that thread id exists there. opencode: used is the last step_finish event's part.tokens input + cache.read + cache.write; opencode reports no window, so window and percent stay blank. pct is the rounded percent when used and a positive window are both counts. The session service writes the four fields to the session-id, context-used, context-window and context-pct columns for every call. A qualified Codex call reads only its composed CODEX_HOME; no rollout from another account is considered.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"The result event's usage counters accumulate over every request the session made, so their sum over the window overstates occupancy. How full a context is depends on what the latest request sent. A missing window is left blank rather than guessed, because a guessed percentage is the input a reset rule would act on.\"\"\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-263)\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3010,"oldLines":7,"newStart":3012,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"Each adapter maps its runner's usage to the pinned OpenTelemetry names\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/session_adapters.py\""," code_symbol = \"OTEL_SEMCONV/USAGE_KEYS/USAGE_COUNT_KEYS/usage_record/_claude_usage/CodexAdapter.usage/OpencodeAdapter.usage\"","-detail = \"\"\"usage(stream) returns one dict with exactly the USAGE_KEYS columns for every runner: cli, semconv (OTEL_SEMCONV, open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai at com
